1
00:00:04,242 --> 00:00:08,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I wanted to bring up the noble method.

2
00:00:11,348 --> 00:00:12,569
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the noble method?

3
00:00:14,370 --> 00:00:25,459
[SPEAKER_00]: And that the wise person, the stream-enterer has attained, or discovered, so to speak.

4
00:00:25,759 --> 00:00:30,743
[SPEAKER_00]: He's understood what the method is for the cultivation of nibbāna.

5
00:00:30,763 --> 00:00:31,384
[SPEAKER_00]: He's understood it?

6
00:00:32,582 --> 00:00:40,786
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that method that now he must exercise so that he can cultivate and develop that nibbāna which he knows?

7
00:00:41,727 --> 00:00:43,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is that noble method?

8
00:00:45,268 --> 00:00:54,273
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the watching, counting of the breath, or watching your feet, or repeating a mantra.

9
00:00:56,334 --> 00:00:56,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is it?

10
00:00:58,615 --> 00:00:59,456
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here's a sutta.

11
00:00:59,996 --> 00:01:01,837
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll put it in the description, the sutta.

12
00:01:02,538 --> 00:01:04,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

13
00:01:05,102 --> 00:01:14,936
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the noble method that he has clearly seen and thoroughly penetrated with wisdom, that he has understood?

14
00:01:15,977 --> 00:01:18,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, householder, the noble disciple

15
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:24,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Attends properly, that yoniso, manasikāra.

16
00:01:25,412 --> 00:01:31,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Says, he attends closely to dependent origination, with this, this is.

17
00:01:32,554 --> 00:01:33,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the... that's it.

18
00:01:36,355 --> 00:01:45,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Paticampāra, doesn't matter how differently you find it described in the suttas, it's always described upon the same principle.

19
00:01:46,730 --> 00:01:50,054
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha himself said, which is, with this, this is.

20
00:01:50,795 --> 00:01:55,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Both the present, both are there, the relationship of determination.

21
00:01:56,521 --> 00:02:05,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason why I'm saying that is because there is a certainly quite established contemporary idea of

22
00:02:06,428 --> 00:02:09,312
[SPEAKER_01]: paticampada being an explanation of rebirth and so on.

23
00:02:09,693 --> 00:02:18,466
[SPEAKER_01]: You can check out other talks on the website about paticampada, but in brief here, it's actually a principle of attending, knowing how to attend things properly.

24
00:02:18,666 --> 00:02:19,708
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what paticampada is.

25
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:28,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the presently enduring necessary condition for the presently enduring necessary state.

26
00:02:29,364 --> 00:02:38,590
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the presently enduring necessary condition for your craving, on account of which you suffer, is a presently enduring feeling, for example.

27
00:02:38,670 --> 00:02:39,871
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with this, this is.

28
00:02:40,591 --> 00:02:42,712
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is the problem.

29
00:02:42,873 --> 00:02:50,117
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is the... the... the... Bhaktiśāsaṃbhāda with the grain, as he was described often.

30
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:55,104
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, by seeing the necessary condition, you get to uproot the problem that is.

31
00:02:55,444 --> 00:02:58,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem of suffering, problem of aging, sickness, birth, and so on.

32
00:03:00,029 --> 00:03:04,473
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean you need to go through all the twelve factors, or sixteen factors, or sometimes eight factors.

33
00:03:04,493 --> 00:03:12,341
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you need to see the principle with clarity, and then it will... that seeing the principle with clarity results in uprooting the asuras.

34
00:03:14,103 --> 00:03:14,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And the...

35
00:03:15,841 --> 00:03:18,802
[SPEAKER_00]: The exercising of that principle is the method.

36
00:03:19,822 --> 00:03:20,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the method.

37
00:03:21,082 --> 00:03:25,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, it's... How can you exercise the principle of right attention?

38
00:03:28,084 --> 00:03:30,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Only by finding out what that principle is?

39
00:03:30,445 --> 00:03:30,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

40
00:03:31,745 --> 00:03:40,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, I mean, like, if you... this method will then result in understanding the clarity of dispassion, disenchantment, and so on.

41
00:03:42,092 --> 00:03:46,953
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't have a method of, oh, these are the prescribed set of motions and things you do.

42
00:03:47,513 --> 00:03:49,834
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then magically arrive at understanding.

43
00:03:51,654 --> 00:03:54,835
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, it's usually interpreted the other way around.

44
00:03:55,395 --> 00:03:58,516
[SPEAKER_00]: I watch my breath, then I get the understanding.

45
00:03:59,076 --> 00:03:59,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

46
00:03:59,896 --> 00:04:03,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And then... And then, basically, the work is done for me.

47
00:04:04,257 --> 00:04:06,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that understanding, that I haven't, like...

48
00:04:07,883 --> 00:04:21,268
[SPEAKER_01]: And even in mundane terms, the only way to develop understanding is to try, well, keep making effort to understand, which means you need to know what needs to be understood, be clear about it, clarify it more, question it, and then you might arrive at understanding.

49
00:04:21,948 --> 00:04:36,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in lay terms, like, not dhamma practice, if a person is not interested in dhamma practice, just lay terms, when it comes to understanding, there is no prescribed sets of just do this, write this down, then look at that, look at that, and it will result in understanding.

50
00:04:37,373 --> 00:04:38,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that even in education.

51
00:04:39,475 --> 00:04:43,838
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be presented with information, you can memorize information, you can learn how information works.

52
00:04:43,878 --> 00:04:47,901
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you understood the principle of it.

53
00:04:47,941 --> 00:04:50,483
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the difference between good students and bad students.

54
00:04:51,063 --> 00:04:59,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, like, students that excel are the ones who actually start understanding things, as opposed to just memorize, have it there, parrot it out and move on.

55
00:05:00,699 --> 00:05:07,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no method of that mechanical nature that can result in understanding automatically.

56
00:05:07,604 --> 00:05:09,686
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just impossible.

57
00:05:11,648 --> 00:05:14,070
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it comes to dhamma, it's even more so.

58
00:05:14,090 --> 00:05:16,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, the understanding is the liberation.

59
00:05:17,152 --> 00:05:19,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoroughly understanding the nature experience means you are an arahant.

60
00:05:21,255 --> 00:05:22,776
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same understanding, by the way.

61
00:05:22,796 --> 00:05:24,618
[SPEAKER_01]: The principle of understanding is still the same.

62
00:05:25,728 --> 00:05:34,500
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not somehow we abandon the reason, rational thought, of what understanding is when it comes to practice of dhamma, and then understanding is something mystical.

63
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,122
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's mystical, it's not understood.

64
00:05:36,243 --> 00:05:36,964
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

65
00:05:37,504 --> 00:05:39,046
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of mysticism.

66
00:05:39,487 --> 00:05:41,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Unclear, ambiguous, mystique.

67
00:05:44,253 --> 00:05:53,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is, when it comes to... like somebody who wants to practice the dhamma, in order to develop the right view, you need to make the effort to understand what the principle of that is.

68
00:05:55,037 --> 00:05:56,158
[SPEAKER_00]: That method.

69
00:05:56,298 --> 00:05:56,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

70
00:05:56,998 --> 00:06:04,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you understand then, ah, that dependent origination or whichever way it's translated, means you got the right view.

71
00:06:05,021 --> 00:06:07,602
[SPEAKER_01]: But... and you also means you know the Four Noble Truths.

72
00:06:08,002 --> 00:06:09,963
[SPEAKER_01]: And it also means you know the way out of suffering.

73
00:06:12,246 --> 00:06:12,906
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

74
00:06:13,086 --> 00:06:17,828
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have that... the idea of that.

75
00:06:17,848 --> 00:06:20,789
[SPEAKER_00]: You can even... you can have the right idea of what right view is.

76
00:06:21,049 --> 00:06:21,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

77
00:06:21,249 --> 00:06:21,870
[SPEAKER_00]: You're very close.

78
00:06:22,610 --> 00:06:25,151
[SPEAKER_01]: But now... That doesn't mean... You need to, actually.

79
00:06:25,231 --> 00:06:26,691
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have the right idea.

80
00:06:27,372 --> 00:06:31,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't have the right idea, how can you then understand the principle behind the idea?

81
00:06:32,617 --> 00:06:37,122
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, you can have the right idea, but if you never make the effort to understand the principle behind it, you won't understand it either.

82
00:06:37,543 --> 00:06:41,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to have the right idea, and then you need to make the effort to understand the principle.

83
00:06:41,427 --> 00:06:44,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might ask, so how do I know when I understood it?

84
00:06:45,192 --> 00:06:48,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it still just an idea or understanding is sort of taking place?

85
00:06:48,776 --> 00:06:51,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you would know if you are unable to suffer.

86
00:06:52,277 --> 00:06:53,118
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

87
00:06:53,158 --> 00:06:59,004
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have the right idea, but you're not going to have the fruits of that right idea immediately.

88
00:06:59,164 --> 00:06:59,565
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

89
00:06:59,865 --> 00:07:00,366
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

90
00:07:00,426 --> 00:07:01,207
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't know.

91
00:07:01,487 --> 00:07:02,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this the right idea?

92
00:07:02,908 --> 00:07:05,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's the most right idea that I could possibly think of.

93
00:07:05,932 --> 00:07:08,254
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the closest that I could think of to be right, yeah.

94
00:07:08,274 --> 00:07:09,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now what?

95
00:07:09,175 --> 00:07:10,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to...

96
00:07:10,376 --> 00:07:11,177
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not enough.

97
00:07:11,237 --> 00:07:11,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Now what?

98
00:07:12,050 --> 00:07:13,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now I'm clear about patisampada.

99
00:07:13,752 --> 00:07:14,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's move on.

100
00:07:14,552 --> 00:07:14,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

101
00:07:14,953 --> 00:07:22,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you're truly clear about patisampada, about the idea of dependent origination, you would be unable to suffer amidst suffering.

102
00:07:22,739 --> 00:07:23,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that the case?

103
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:25,641
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to ask yourself honestly and transparently.

104
00:07:26,522 --> 00:07:32,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I unable to suffer amidst things that used to make me suffer and trigger emotions and so on?

105
00:07:33,488 --> 00:07:36,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I just am unable to suffer.

106
00:07:36,230 --> 00:07:40,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like I prevent myself from experiencing these things or try to hide away or

107
00:07:41,457 --> 00:07:42,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Explain them to myself.

108
00:07:42,698 --> 00:07:44,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's it's, you know, it's impermanent.

109
00:07:44,239 --> 00:07:45,020
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a big deal.

110
00:07:45,260 --> 00:07:45,500
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

111
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:48,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I... how much am I affected by it in the first place?

112
00:07:49,102 --> 00:07:51,844
[SPEAKER_01]: That should be a measure of whether you're progressing rightly or not.

113
00:07:52,785 --> 00:07:59,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you are understanding Patitsa Upādāna, Four Noble Truths, means you you will be unable to be affected as much as before.

114
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:02,872
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you start reckoning, then the immediately confidence comes.

115
00:08:03,072 --> 00:08:04,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, I am unable.

116
00:08:05,832 --> 00:08:15,086
[SPEAKER_01]: To be disturbed, not because I'm trying to hide myself away from the disturbance, it's simply because I'm... basically, I'm above the disturbance, on account of my understanding.

117
00:08:20,873 --> 00:08:21,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

118
00:08:21,333 --> 00:08:24,034
[SPEAKER_00]: How does my understanding relate to my suffering?

119
00:08:24,434 --> 00:08:26,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it... Not even relate.

120
00:08:26,555 --> 00:08:27,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then, you know, how does it relate?

121
00:08:27,895 --> 00:08:29,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me figure it out how it relates.

122
00:08:30,056 --> 00:08:34,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is my understanding of... are these ideas that I have, are they freeing me?

123
00:08:34,797 --> 00:08:38,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they preventing me from being able to be disturbed by the suffering in the first place?

124
00:08:39,238 --> 00:08:41,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Regardless of whether I know the connection between the two or not.

125
00:08:42,759 --> 00:08:43,140
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

126
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:44,280
[SPEAKER_01]: How much can I suffer?

127
00:08:47,873 --> 00:08:50,434
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're understanding things, that should be diminishing.

128
00:08:51,174 --> 00:08:57,656
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't say exactly to what... quantify the extent, but you know that you are less disturbed.

129
00:08:58,096 --> 00:09:01,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Disturbance persists less and less, arises less frequently.

130
00:09:02,177 --> 00:09:07,079
[SPEAKER_00]: But how do you know that it is the right idea when you have no results?

131
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:09,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, you don't.

132
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:11,281
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the faith comes in.

133
00:09:11,301 --> 00:09:12,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I suffer anymore?

134
00:09:12,022 --> 00:09:13,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

135
00:09:13,162 --> 00:09:13,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there prosperity?

136
00:09:13,843 --> 00:09:14,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, maybe.

137
00:09:14,523 --> 00:09:15,684
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we keep saying that.

138
00:09:15,864 --> 00:09:18,785
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's impossible to become a sotāpanna accidentally.

139
00:09:20,306 --> 00:09:21,667
[SPEAKER_01]: To get the right view accidentally.

140
00:09:21,827 --> 00:09:24,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, I've just done this and wow, now I know.

141
00:09:24,969 --> 00:09:28,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have no criteria, you have no immediate measure.

142
00:09:29,197 --> 00:09:37,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why would you then be doing that thing when you can't even discern it as something to be done, let alone keep repeating it without the apparent result?

143
00:09:37,164 --> 00:09:41,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, keep... Why would you keep repeating that right view?

144
00:09:41,648 --> 00:09:43,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you need to repeat it.

145
00:09:43,250 --> 00:09:48,855
[SPEAKER_01]: That idea that you clarify once, you need to repeat maybe a million of times, depending on where you are at internally.

146
00:09:49,736 --> 00:09:50,757
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to keep repeating it.

147
00:09:50,797 --> 00:09:53,099
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason you'll be repeating it is out of faith.

148
00:09:54,165 --> 00:10:00,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of sufficient faith in that, like, intellectual recognition that this might be closest to the truth.

149
00:10:00,706 --> 00:10:05,307
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to verify that is to commit and start repeating it and re-drilling.

150
00:10:05,447 --> 00:10:06,148
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the training.

151
00:10:06,208 --> 00:10:07,208
[SPEAKER_01]: He trains himself thus.

152
00:10:07,648 --> 00:10:08,888
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not regard this as mine.

153
00:10:09,008 --> 00:10:10,309
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not regard this as permanent.

154
00:10:10,609 --> 00:10:11,669
[SPEAKER_01]: He... on and on.

155
00:10:11,689 --> 00:10:13,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like once or twice or three times.

156
00:10:14,269 --> 00:10:15,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Who knows how many times?

157
00:10:15,610 --> 00:10:16,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the Buddha says how many times.

158
00:10:17,929 --> 00:10:22,512
[SPEAKER_01]: For seven years, seven months, who knows, but basically you just... you can't do it.

159
00:10:22,552 --> 00:10:23,252
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll do it this much.

160
00:10:23,272 --> 00:10:25,494
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to do it until the results take place.

161
00:10:25,514 --> 00:10:28,396
[SPEAKER_00]: But he also... he gives different ways to do that.

162
00:10:28,416 --> 00:10:29,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, sure.

163
00:10:29,256 --> 00:10:31,398
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's doing the same principle, doing the same thing.

164
00:10:32,078 --> 00:10:35,620
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can do it and then that's where satipaṭṭhāna comes in.

165
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:36,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

166
00:10:36,521 --> 00:10:43,906
[SPEAKER_01]: You can either attend to the body... You can do it on the basis of the body, the principle, you can do it on the basis of feelings, on the basis of the mind, on the basis of the thoughts.

167
00:10:46,758 --> 00:10:48,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you tend to the breath.

168
00:10:48,479 --> 00:10:49,900
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the principle you're looking for.

169
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:53,683
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, when you practice mindfulness of the body, you know, oh, I'm watching sensations.

170
00:10:53,703 --> 00:10:58,165
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's just observing sense of touch, sensations of touch, perception of touch.

171
00:10:59,226 --> 00:11:00,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensations through my legs and back.

172
00:11:01,007 --> 00:11:03,909
[SPEAKER_01]: That's literally perception of touch, nothing else.

173
00:11:03,929 --> 00:11:08,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So, somehow, perception of touch results in this transcendental experience of understanding.

174
00:11:09,198 --> 00:11:09,358
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

175
00:11:09,818 --> 00:11:12,679
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just mechanically chasing for the novelty experiences.

176
00:11:13,419 --> 00:11:14,399
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what most people's meditation is.

177
00:11:14,419 --> 00:11:15,680
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not exercising your right view.

178
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:18,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You just... You're not exercising your right view because you don't have it.

179
00:11:18,820 --> 00:11:21,001
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not going to have it because you're not looking to find it.

180
00:11:21,301 --> 00:11:24,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And you find it where you understand the principle of paticca upādāna.

181
00:11:25,822 --> 00:11:35,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's when Ananda, who, you know, who had the right view for like over 20 years, when he was sitting that time with the Buddha and said, I mean, it's amazing, like, but it's the clearest thing possible.

182
00:11:35,224 --> 00:11:35,905
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there in your

183
00:11:38,061 --> 00:11:39,142
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, it's so easy.

184
00:11:39,222 --> 00:11:39,803
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always there.

185
00:11:40,324 --> 00:11:42,687
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, no, don't say that it's easy.

186
00:11:42,707 --> 00:11:48,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's easy for you, because you've been, basically, you understood it 20 years ago, and ever since, you've just been fortifying it.

187
00:11:49,034 --> 00:11:54,020
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's because of that very thing, that paticcaṁ pāda principle, that people don't understand.

188
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:57,564
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why all the eons of suffering and saṃsaras and everything else.

189
00:11:58,588 --> 00:11:59,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, it is.

190
00:12:00,008 --> 00:12:02,930
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the closest thing to you, and that's why it's invisible.

191
00:12:02,970 --> 00:12:06,691
[SPEAKER_01]: People are not used to looking so closely, so to speak, not attending so closely.

192
00:12:07,191 --> 00:12:15,174
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say attending closely, I don't mean attending to the particular details, but more like, wait, I'm attending to this on account of what?

193
00:12:15,394 --> 00:12:18,015
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the peripheral of this currently, or is it attention?

194
00:12:18,055 --> 00:12:19,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Perspective on your attention.

195
00:12:19,395 --> 00:12:24,117
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the perspective on account of... Yeah, what's the perspective on things that I'm currently attending to?

196
00:12:25,862 --> 00:12:28,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you're feeling, for example.

197
00:12:29,105 --> 00:12:30,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm feeling upset.

198
00:12:32,348 --> 00:12:43,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, you can now choose a direction of attending the upset, and what has seemingly caused it, what you perceived at the time, who said what, when, what you should do next time, how you should decide it.

199
00:12:44,018 --> 00:12:50,624
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is already within the context that you're affected by the upset, it's unpleasant, and now you're trying to get rid of this pleasure.

200
00:12:51,860 --> 00:12:52,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can then stop.

201
00:12:53,221 --> 00:12:53,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait.

202
00:12:53,741 --> 00:13:06,870
[SPEAKER_01]: I... how many times I chased down... psychology... psychologizing of why I got upset, I should have done this, should have done that, next time I'll do this, all in order to prevent that upset happening to you.

203
00:13:06,950 --> 00:13:07,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can choose to attend.

204
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:08,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait.

205
00:13:08,291 --> 00:13:15,215
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I don't give in to trying to get rid of the pain of upset, but see what else is there as a necessary basis for that upset to take place?

206
00:13:16,116 --> 00:13:17,296
[SPEAKER_01]: That's now the different direction.

207
00:13:17,336 --> 00:13:20,939
[SPEAKER_01]: That's direction of broader context on account of currently there is an upset.

208
00:13:21,937 --> 00:13:33,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Which then starts including your need to get rid of the upset, because it's unpleasant, which you won't be seeing if you just give in and follow the thought of upset down the line, wherever it takes you.

209
00:13:35,029 --> 00:13:42,656
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the context, it's kind of rediscovered on a broader terms, because there's always a context.

210
00:13:43,217 --> 00:13:47,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you choose to psychologize, the context is trying to get rid of the pain, but you're unaware of it.

211
00:13:49,238 --> 00:14:00,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no limit to how much you can develop context rightly, but doesn't matter how much you develop the context, or how much you lose the context, the principle of with context, this is the direction, stays the same.

212
00:14:01,122 --> 00:14:02,282
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

213
00:14:03,403 --> 00:14:08,825
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just want to reverse the order, so to speak, and not go with the grain, as the Buddha would say.

214
00:14:08,885 --> 00:14:12,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Context of upset means get rid of the upset, for an untrained mind.

215
00:14:13,625 --> 00:14:15,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you learn that that doesn't work.

216
00:14:15,628 --> 00:14:18,251
[SPEAKER_01]: You use your intelligence, your reason, to see that that doesn't work.

217
00:14:18,271 --> 00:14:19,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if I try the opposite?

218
00:14:20,074 --> 00:14:27,804
[SPEAKER_01]: So, context of upset, need to get rid of the upset, restrain the need to get rid of the upset within the context of upset.

219
00:14:28,124 --> 00:14:31,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start swimming against the grain, against the current.

220
00:14:33,521 --> 00:14:40,028
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the more you do that, the more familiar then you become with the context of upset that you were blindly just trying to get rid of.

221
00:14:40,649 --> 00:14:43,552
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, well, wait, the only reason I'm upset...

222
00:14:44,368 --> 00:14:50,870
[SPEAKER_01]: That the only reason I suffer on account of this upset, is because I'm trying to get rid of the upset, out of my view that this must be removed.

223
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:55,411
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the perspective that you... That's the perspective you gain, if you start swimming upstream.

224
00:14:56,151 --> 00:14:57,751
[SPEAKER_01]: But for that, you need that restraint.

225
00:14:58,192 --> 00:15:03,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, you need faith, because you have no verification that upstream will guarantee you results of freedom from upset.

226
00:15:04,618 --> 00:15:18,083
[SPEAKER_01]: But that faith, how much of that faith you require, is determined of how much of your intellectual capacity and reasoning you have, on account of which you understood that this is most likely to make sense.

227
00:15:18,103 --> 00:15:19,863
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like, hmm...

228
00:15:19,883 --> 00:15:26,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Induce certain, on a base of reasoning, induce like a... as a premise, which is the safest one.

229
00:15:26,706 --> 00:15:28,426
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't know for sure beforehand.

230
00:15:29,147 --> 00:15:32,628
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the insufficiency of reason.

231
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:36,942
[SPEAKER_01]: By itself, you cannot reason the extent of why you should repeat the reason.

232
00:15:39,704 --> 00:15:48,069
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to have faith that your reasoning was accurate enough to keep repeating it, and then you get the results, the direct results of repetition of reason.

233
00:15:48,530 --> 00:15:51,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just once reason, twice reason, three times reason.

234
00:15:52,012 --> 00:15:55,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Like repeating the same reflection, and drilling it, and drilling it, and making it persist.

235
00:15:57,215 --> 00:16:00,017
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that dhamma, if you do it, is beyond reason.

236
00:16:00,866 --> 00:16:12,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because it's unreasonable, but because you need to reasonably and rationally recognize what is for your welfare, what is good, what should be, like, heeded to and done.

237
00:16:13,373 --> 00:16:14,533
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you need to repeat it.

238
00:16:15,094 --> 00:16:16,194
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't reason that.

239
00:16:16,394 --> 00:16:25,860
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're, like, pure logical mind that just reasons, reasons accurately, you can't reason why you should repeat this same reflection, same reasoning for seven years.

240
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:29,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no immediate result that can then give you a feedback for your reason.

241
00:16:30,917 --> 00:16:47,705
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do it out of faith, without sacrificing your reason, which, as we made apparent, it's actually necessary, so, you don't sacrifice the reason, you even, like, take it even further, and just keep repeating the same reason, out of faith, you'll get the result of the right view.

242
00:16:49,826 --> 00:16:50,286
[SPEAKER_01]: How long?

243
00:16:50,326 --> 00:16:52,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's where the faith comes in.

244
00:16:54,532 --> 00:16:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you come to see a doctor.

245
00:16:56,915 --> 00:16:58,117
[SPEAKER_01]: You tried so many doctors.

246
00:16:58,758 --> 00:17:01,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, you heard about such and such doctor and you haven't tried that treatment.

247
00:17:01,982 --> 00:17:11,634
[SPEAKER_01]: And based on kind of trial and error that you learn on account of trying other doctors, you sort of induce a conclusion that there is a good chance that he might be right.

248
00:17:12,591 --> 00:17:14,972
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't fit into anything wrong that you've done before.

249
00:17:15,312 --> 00:17:16,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Seems to be the opposite.

250
00:17:16,973 --> 00:17:20,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can go and... you kind of... you can reason that it makes sense.

251
00:17:21,215 --> 00:17:23,016
[SPEAKER_01]: But that in itself will not cure you.

252
00:17:23,196 --> 00:17:26,057
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to go and start doing what the doctor prescribed.

253
00:17:26,898 --> 00:17:29,339
[SPEAKER_01]: And you cannot reason that that would be the right thing to do.

254
00:17:30,019 --> 00:17:36,582
[SPEAKER_01]: You can reason that it's going to be the least likely thing that's wrong, but you need to start doing it to see whether it actually cures you or not.

255
00:17:37,323 --> 00:17:39,424
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly... And when it cures you,

256
00:17:40,732 --> 00:17:48,337
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you're now healthy, cured, has not been... you haven't obtained it by mere reasoning alone, as the Buddha said.

257
00:17:49,118 --> 00:17:51,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma cannot be obtained by mere reasoning alone.

258
00:17:51,519 --> 00:17:52,980
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what ātākavacāra means.

259
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:54,381
[SPEAKER_01]: It's beyond mere reason.

260
00:17:55,582 --> 00:18:02,527
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you use the reason, and then you keep repeating the reason, and then you get cured, and now you're... you'd have not arrived there.

261
00:18:02,547 --> 00:18:07,230
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be true to say, oh, I arrived at this cure simply by reasoning that that's the cure.

262
00:18:08,303 --> 00:18:08,423
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

263
00:18:08,503 --> 00:18:09,865
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what brought you to the cure.

264
00:18:10,446 --> 00:18:13,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Clear, authentic, transparent reasoning.

265
00:18:13,709 --> 00:18:16,372
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, repetition of that cure is what cure you.

266
00:18:16,733 --> 00:18:17,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Repetition of the reason.

267
00:18:18,014 --> 00:18:18,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Repetition of the reason.

268
00:18:19,036 --> 00:18:19,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

269
00:18:19,696 --> 00:18:19,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

270
00:18:20,317 --> 00:18:24,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you reason the principle, the necessity of craving for suffering.

271
00:18:25,223 --> 00:18:28,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, did you have a say in how your feelings arise?

272
00:18:29,700 --> 00:18:29,820
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

273
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:31,501
[SPEAKER_01]: You just resist them, or want them.

274
00:18:31,621 --> 00:18:32,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

275
00:18:32,721 --> 00:18:35,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, use your intellect, your reason, to clarify reflection of it.

276
00:18:36,442 --> 00:18:41,244
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, you get a sense of, okay, so when the feeling arises, if it's pleasant, I should not lean towards it.

277
00:18:41,284 --> 00:18:42,924
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's unpleasant, I should lean away from it.

278
00:18:43,324 --> 00:18:51,427
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I make the effort now, to maintain that as a context, as a reflection, as a state of mind,

279
00:18:52,535 --> 00:18:55,017
[SPEAKER_01]: That I arrived at on account of reasoning it rightly.

280
00:18:55,817 --> 00:18:59,780
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do maintain it, you will approve the... you're swimming basically against the craving.

281
00:19:00,680 --> 00:19:07,545
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you reach the sufficient point of diminishing of craving, you will then recognize it, and that's where you've verified your reason for yourself.

282
00:19:08,525 --> 00:19:09,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpatti.

283
00:19:09,326 --> 00:19:09,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

284
00:19:09,926 --> 00:19:12,488
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, ah, nobody can be my guide anymore.

285
00:19:12,508 --> 00:19:16,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you have discovered the principle with this, this is, and that is your guide.

286
00:19:17,851 --> 00:19:19,052
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no doubt anymore.

287
00:19:19,652 --> 00:19:20,433
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot doubt that.

288
00:19:21,563 --> 00:19:22,304
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot doubt that.

289
00:19:28,328 --> 00:19:30,249
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is a noble method.

290
00:19:30,329 --> 00:19:34,872
[SPEAKER_01]: The closest you can say to a method for understanding is trying to understand.

291
00:19:35,773 --> 00:19:36,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to understand what?

292
00:19:36,713 --> 00:19:37,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Good question.

293
00:19:37,334 --> 00:19:39,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Read the suttas, you'll see what you need to understand.

294
00:19:42,370 --> 00:19:43,351
[SPEAKER_00]: And then repeating it.

295
00:19:43,411 --> 00:19:44,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And then keep doing it.

296
00:19:44,412 --> 00:19:55,044
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, the measure of understanding is not when you get a clear idea about understanding, it's when you cannot suffer anymore, when you have no passion left anymore.

297
00:19:55,084 --> 00:19:57,287
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the measure of the extent of your understanding.

298
00:20:00,083 --> 00:20:04,926
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the paññavimutti, arahant, the type of practice... well, type.

299
00:20:05,486 --> 00:20:09,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, certain people arrive at arahantship through, as the Buddha would say, resolve upon wisdom.

300
00:20:10,509 --> 00:20:17,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, you have such a clarity of insight and wisdom, but you haven't done it long enough to uproot the asavas in you.

301
00:20:18,473 --> 00:20:21,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Like sotāpāna, sakadāgāmī, anāgāmī, they haven't done it long enough.

302
00:20:22,732 --> 00:20:27,676
[SPEAKER_01]: So, some people would just stick to it, like, you know, with a bit of...

303
00:20:27,696 --> 00:20:38,406
[SPEAKER_01]: They know out of faith now that they have confirmed, so they're confident that if they stick to these reflections, this kind of practice, it will result in uprooting greed, aversion, delusion, because they've already seen it for themselves to some extent.

304
00:20:39,006 --> 00:20:49,569
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, the other type is who, like, emphasize that rational clarity of that principle, and then, literally, make a determination upon maintaining it at any expense, at every other expense.

305
00:20:50,169 --> 00:20:55,890
[SPEAKER_01]: They just maintain the context of where this, this is, or any other aspect of that wisdom.

306
00:20:56,490 --> 00:21:01,832
[SPEAKER_01]: So, resolved upon that wisdom, long enough, you are an arahant.

307
00:21:02,232 --> 00:21:06,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, resolved upon the clarity of that idea, long enough, you will be an arahant.

308
00:21:07,713 --> 00:21:09,975
[SPEAKER_00]: They maintain mindfulness of that...

309
00:21:09,995 --> 00:21:10,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

310
00:21:10,415 --> 00:21:11,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that idea.

311
00:21:11,335 --> 00:21:11,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

312
00:21:11,796 --> 00:21:13,937
[SPEAKER_01]: They maintain sensory strength through that idea.

313
00:21:14,217 --> 00:21:15,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Samādhi through that idea.

314
00:21:18,099 --> 00:21:23,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maintaining that idea is composing... keeping that idea composed, which is a definition of samādhi.

315
00:21:30,287 --> 00:21:32,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... that's what means dhamma is beyond reason.

316
00:21:35,812 --> 00:21:43,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Beyond reason means, as the Buddha said, not obtainable by reason alone or by mere reasoning.

317
00:21:43,780 --> 00:21:47,785
[SPEAKER_01]: But often people will jump into conclusion, ah, the dhamma cannot be reasoned.

318
00:21:49,344 --> 00:21:50,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, dhamma is unreasonable.

319
00:21:50,645 --> 00:21:52,005
[SPEAKER_01]: So, make it mystical and ambiguous.

320
00:21:52,066 --> 00:21:55,287
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you just need to read the suttas and you see it can very much be reasoned.

321
00:21:55,908 --> 00:22:01,571
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would often say, well, if the other brahmins and ascetics ask you this, you should then, in return, ask them that.

322
00:22:02,151 --> 00:22:04,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if that's your premise, why is this your conclusion?

323
00:22:04,552 --> 00:22:07,294
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, it's just pure, clear, authentic reasoning.

324
00:22:07,994 --> 00:22:17,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you just look for faults, or you want to defend yourself, like, irrationally, but because you rationally understand the place where they come from, and you're rationally understanding why it's inadequate.

325
00:22:18,421 --> 00:22:20,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when, when Jains would claim they're omniscient.

326
00:22:21,303 --> 00:22:23,665
[SPEAKER_01]: They see and hear, they see and know all the time.

327
00:22:24,565 --> 00:22:33,011
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha would say, well, if somebody claims that, you should ask them, why that follower of that teaching, who see and hear, who see and know everything all the time, why do they get into accidents?

328
00:22:33,511 --> 00:22:34,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do they fall into pits?

329
00:22:34,812 --> 00:22:36,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do they get attacked by dogs?

330
00:22:37,527 --> 00:22:40,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they would have foreseen these things, and they would have avoided it.

331
00:22:40,349 --> 00:22:41,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody would avoid those things.

332
00:22:41,970 --> 00:22:46,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they try to say, well, no, it's because, you know, they choose to go, whatever.

333
00:22:47,034 --> 00:22:50,756
[SPEAKER_01]: They try to, like now, irrationally rationalize it.

334
00:22:52,197 --> 00:22:57,621
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you read the suttas, and it's very much reasonable, but that doesn't mean that mere reason is enough.

335
00:22:58,922 --> 00:23:03,786
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when that logician read the suttas, Nirvāyācārya mentions it, he said,

336
00:23:05,123 --> 00:23:06,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is just mere tautology.

337
00:23:06,945 --> 00:23:09,346
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole suttas are just tautological statements.

338
00:23:10,507 --> 00:23:19,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is true, if you never think there is anything more than just getting the idea of understanding that stands for understanding.

339
00:23:19,515 --> 00:23:23,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they are the tautological ideas.

340
00:23:23,338 --> 00:23:29,183
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're free from craving, you will not be a victim to craving anymore.

341
00:23:29,203 --> 00:23:29,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

342
00:23:29,703 --> 00:23:31,544
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get that on the level of idea.

343
00:23:32,387 --> 00:23:39,891
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have no faith now to assume that there is maybe more to that idea, as in more to be developed, you'll stop there.

344
00:23:40,351 --> 00:23:46,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what like a person who puts reason and only reason as the sole criteria can never arrive at dhamma.

345
00:23:47,654 --> 00:23:51,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, the idea of it is perfectly reasonable and self-explanatory almost.

346
00:23:52,056 --> 00:23:53,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So what else is missing there?

347
00:23:53,777 --> 00:23:55,998
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the repetition of that idea that misses.

348
00:23:56,618 --> 00:23:59,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your experience is still experience of craving.

349
00:24:00,757 --> 00:24:03,398
[SPEAKER_01]: With craving, with passion, with greed, with aversion, with delusion.

350
00:24:03,999 --> 00:24:10,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have the idea that absence of craving means you're not liable to the pressure of craving anymore, which means you will not suffer on account of it.

351
00:24:11,263 --> 00:24:15,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so now the idea that I have, clear idea of craving is to be abandoned.

352
00:24:16,706 --> 00:24:18,967
[SPEAKER_01]: I must actually cultivate bhāvanā.

353
00:24:19,487 --> 00:24:21,088
[SPEAKER_01]: I must keep repeating it.

354
00:24:21,108 --> 00:24:26,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just get the idea and be... and take it for granted that now that has been completely understood.

355
00:24:27,588 --> 00:24:35,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what a person, as I said, who puts rationalizing and thinking first and foremost, can never arrive at.

356
00:24:35,918 --> 00:24:41,143
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they can never commit to any degree of faith, even if it's literally a faith to a doctor.

357
00:24:41,744 --> 00:24:47,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody's asking you to... like, the problem with other religions is when they ask for faith, they ask it at the expense of reason.

358
00:24:49,101 --> 00:24:52,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, don't... just don't question this.

359
00:24:52,302 --> 00:24:52,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Have faith.

360
00:24:53,603 --> 00:24:54,763
[SPEAKER_01]: I abandon your reason.

361
00:24:55,584 --> 00:24:59,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if... so, accept contradictions, accept mysticism, accept every other nonsense.

362
00:25:00,746 --> 00:25:02,067
[SPEAKER_01]: This is not the faith we speak about.

363
00:25:02,087 --> 00:25:03,007
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the faith of wait.

364
00:25:03,687 --> 00:25:04,208
[SPEAKER_01]: I am ill.

365
00:25:04,368 --> 00:25:05,308
[SPEAKER_01]: I recognize that much.

366
00:25:05,428 --> 00:25:08,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I am bothered by suffering, bothered by greed, aversion, delusion.

367
00:25:08,770 --> 00:25:11,751
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to place faith in a doctor to some extent.

368
00:25:11,911 --> 00:25:14,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you will not take the medicine because you don't trust him.

369
00:25:15,196 --> 00:25:27,820
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you think that that's the ultimate criteria of not trusting anything and everything, well, you're free to do so, but then you will remain liable to greed, aversion, delusion, that you kind of recognize as a problem that must be addressed, a form of illness.

370
00:25:28,620 --> 00:25:38,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, people who are like purely and only reasonable and rational, they'll never even accept greed, aversion, delusion to be the problem.

371
00:25:38,143 --> 00:25:39,103
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll just reason it out.

372
00:25:40,264 --> 00:25:42,064
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reasoning cannot free you from suffering.

373
00:25:42,364 --> 00:25:44,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Mere reasoning cannot free you from suffering.

374
00:25:45,129 --> 00:25:55,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're truly honest with yourself, it doesn't matter how much... how great your reasoning and rationalizing is, when you suffer, you realize there is a problem.

375
00:25:55,477 --> 00:25:58,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And, again, you can use the reasoning to realize that suffering is a problem.

376
00:25:59,861 --> 00:26:05,785
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people, when it comes to suffering, would use the reasoning to, like, deal with suffering, not look at it for what it is.

377
00:26:07,447 --> 00:26:11,450
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, as we spoke before, you need reasoning, like objective thinking,

378
00:26:12,433 --> 00:26:16,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarity of that rational thought paired with existential attitude.

379
00:26:16,596 --> 00:26:19,398
[SPEAKER_01]: And existential attitude means existence is a problem.

380
00:26:20,659 --> 00:26:22,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is a problem.

381
00:26:23,201 --> 00:26:30,786
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you use the rational thought and your reasoning to unravel how and why this existence is a problem.

382
00:26:32,107 --> 00:26:32,788
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you would.

383
00:26:32,848 --> 00:26:36,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a problem because you're not in control, because you're subject to greed, aversion, delusion.

384
00:26:36,950 --> 00:26:40,013
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subject to death, accidents, sickness, aging.

385
00:26:41,183 --> 00:26:43,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Unavoidable and those things are unpleasant.

386
00:26:43,104 --> 00:26:44,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're subject to suffering.

387
00:26:46,127 --> 00:26:50,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Your reason is subject to suffering.

388
00:26:50,510 --> 00:26:51,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I uproot this?

389
00:26:52,031 --> 00:26:54,072
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I cure myself from that problem?

390
00:26:55,533 --> 00:27:02,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by abandoning reason and pretending, you know, the universe, universal energy, that's all I need to tap into and I'm done.

391
00:27:02,058 --> 00:27:02,198
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

392
00:27:02,859 --> 00:27:08,483
[SPEAKER_01]: By using the reason to find the right doctor and then by using the reason to keep applying the instructions from the right doctor.

393
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:10,340
[SPEAKER_01]: And who is the right doctor?

394
00:27:10,380 --> 00:27:18,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you have no criteria for that, as we said before, but you can use your reason to determine who is the least likely gonna be the wrong doctor.

395
00:27:18,602 --> 00:27:21,442
[SPEAKER_01]: And then start applying it, trial and error, and then you see.

396
00:27:22,402 --> 00:27:23,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this diminishing the suffering?

397
00:27:23,803 --> 00:27:26,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this diminishing liability to be disturbed?

398
00:27:27,823 --> 00:27:29,564
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure, but maybe I should apply more?

399
00:27:30,124 --> 00:27:30,924
[SPEAKER_01]: No, well, hold on.

400
00:27:31,064 --> 00:27:33,944
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not sure, make the effort to understand why you're not sure.

401
00:27:34,545 --> 00:27:35,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because you haven't applied enough?

402
00:27:36,345 --> 00:27:36,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

403
00:27:37,405 --> 00:27:38,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because it's inadequate?

404
00:27:38,826 --> 00:27:41,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, now you discover contradiction in the doctor's instruction.

405
00:27:42,428 --> 00:27:43,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, that's why.

406
00:27:43,669 --> 00:27:44,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Change the doctor.

407
00:27:45,370 --> 00:27:49,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you discover contradiction in your understanding of doctor's instruction.

408
00:27:50,873 --> 00:27:51,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Change your understanding.

409
00:27:51,914 --> 00:27:52,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Upgrade it.

410
00:27:52,454 --> 00:27:52,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarify it more.

411
00:27:52,915 --> 00:27:55,356
[SPEAKER_00]: You can ask your doctor, why should I take this medicine?

412
00:27:55,596 --> 00:27:56,997
[SPEAKER_00]: How well just... Yeah.

413
00:27:57,437 --> 00:27:58,998
[SPEAKER_01]: What people do, why should I do that?

414
00:27:59,138 --> 00:28:00,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you... like, I can't answer that for you.

415
00:28:02,093 --> 00:28:06,416
[SPEAKER_01]: I can tell you why you should do it, but that will... that cannot be the reason why you should do it.

416
00:28:06,537 --> 00:28:09,499
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason why you should do it is if you start seeing your existence as a problem.

417
00:28:09,839 --> 00:28:11,901
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the reason why you should do it.

418
00:28:11,941 --> 00:28:14,443
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, then you question, what am I asked to do?

419
00:28:15,674 --> 00:28:27,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I asked to abandon my reason, to abandon my intellect, to sacrifice my basically rational thought and commit to this ridiculous, like, just mere faith alone, like, salvation?

420
00:28:28,262 --> 00:28:28,902
[SPEAKER_01]: No, not at all.

421
00:28:29,503 --> 00:28:40,530
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm actually asked to use the reason and turn it against the existence, to the point of, like, revealing that underlying nature of existence, which is nature of suffering.

422
00:28:41,827 --> 00:28:44,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you will not need any other proof why you should do it.

423
00:28:44,629 --> 00:28:46,770
[SPEAKER_01]: You will recognize suffering is a problem.

424
00:28:46,810 --> 00:28:47,891
[SPEAKER_01]: We are liable to this.

425
00:28:48,971 --> 00:28:49,652
[SPEAKER_01]: It's perilous.

426
00:28:50,092 --> 00:28:51,313
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dangerous.

427
00:28:51,333 --> 00:28:53,214
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually worth escaping from.

428
00:28:53,934 --> 00:29:00,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that, you need to have reason and faith to arrive at that mundane conclusion that you should practice the dhamma.

429
00:29:01,619 --> 00:29:08,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, yeah, you are fortunate if you already have faith to practice the dhamma, but that in itself... Oh, yes, I have faith that that's the best doctor.

430
00:29:09,364 --> 00:29:11,725
[SPEAKER_01]: But are you now reasonably applying his instruction?

431
00:29:13,685 --> 00:29:20,607
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I just hope through my admiration of doctor and my belief that he's the greatest doctor, I'll somehow be cured.

432
00:29:21,107 --> 00:29:21,267
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

433
00:29:21,967 --> 00:29:29,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So the faith type, when the Buddha talks about, is the type that has a lot of faith, so he doesn't necessarily question the doctor to the same extent.

434
00:29:29,589 --> 00:29:32,109
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean he's unreasonable, even the faith type.

435
00:29:32,730 --> 00:29:36,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you still need reason to apply the principle of the right method.

436
00:29:37,231 --> 00:29:38,291
[SPEAKER_00]: With this, this is.

437
00:29:39,017 --> 00:29:40,697
[SPEAKER_00]: He gets a sufficient amount of reason.

438
00:29:40,878 --> 00:29:41,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

439
00:29:41,358 --> 00:29:41,838
[SPEAKER_01]: He applies it.

440
00:29:41,898 --> 00:29:42,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

441
00:29:42,358 --> 00:29:50,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he gets faith that compensates for maybe slightly lesser extent of reason, but there is still reason and reasonable attention and recognition of these things.

442
00:29:50,780 --> 00:29:51,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, how can you apply them?

443
00:29:53,521 --> 00:29:54,781
[SPEAKER_00]: When you don't know what you're applying.

444
00:29:54,821 --> 00:29:55,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

445
00:29:55,362 --> 00:29:55,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

446
00:29:55,742 --> 00:29:55,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

447
00:29:56,622 --> 00:29:57,182
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I'm saying.

448
00:29:57,202 --> 00:30:07,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, being overly rational, as in never having any any degree of faith in regard to it and seeing that there is maybe a longer, greater perspective,

449
00:30:08,582 --> 00:30:10,123
[SPEAKER_01]: What have you understood there?

450
00:30:10,143 --> 00:30:15,228
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just parroting, basically, the mantra.

451
00:30:31,420 --> 00:30:34,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there understanding of the principle behind on account which you can't suffer?

452
00:30:34,521 --> 00:30:39,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it's just stop thinking, stop thinking, be here now, just stop thinking, just present moment, at present moment.

453
00:30:40,383 --> 00:30:42,464
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're denying even the basic reason there.

454
00:30:43,124 --> 00:30:43,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

455
00:30:44,104 --> 00:30:48,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Not being obtainable by mere reasoning alone doesn't mean you need to become unreasonable.

456
00:30:50,486 --> 00:30:56,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Needs to... you need to use the reason to the point of then you get the results that come from beyond reason.

457
00:30:56,908 --> 00:30:57,688
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's beyond reason?

458
00:30:57,708 --> 00:30:59,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's the repetition of reason is beyond reason.

459
00:31:03,273 --> 00:31:07,496
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you won't repeat the reason, if you don't see the point of reason, such as mysticism.

460
00:31:07,716 --> 00:31:08,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop thinking, be here now.

461
00:31:09,497 --> 00:31:17,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And you won't repeat the reason, if you never abandon your reason as a starting point, to the point of never allow any degree of faith in regard to it.

462
00:31:19,423 --> 00:31:21,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith means what you put first, basically.

463
00:31:22,187 --> 00:31:29,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I have a criteria of what the right doctor is, what the right doctor isn't, but the fact is I'm not cured.

464
00:31:29,429 --> 00:31:30,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm still sick.

465
00:31:30,769 --> 00:31:37,591
[SPEAKER_01]: But on account of my criteria being put first as fore and foremost, I will not commit to any doctor's instruction.

466
00:31:38,571 --> 00:31:41,292
[SPEAKER_01]: So your own reasoning impeded you to go beyond reason.

467
00:31:43,132 --> 00:31:48,594
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize, well, I have nothing to lose, because I'm not sacrificing reason, I'm not being asked to do unwholesome things,

468
00:31:49,106 --> 00:32:03,029
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I just for this, you know, give a benefit of doubt to the doctor that is least likely to be wrong, based on my own reasoning, and then follow his prescription, and I'll see if I'm getting better or not.

469
00:32:03,169 --> 00:32:05,070
[SPEAKER_01]: I have nothing to lose.

470
00:32:05,090 --> 00:32:13,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if I ask you, on account of following my medicine, go and do these hideous acts and these crazy things, but believe you'll be right, then you use your reason.

471
00:32:13,432 --> 00:32:16,112
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha referred to like the mundane right view.

472
00:32:16,773 --> 00:32:18,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing there is suffering.

473
00:32:18,901 --> 00:32:21,942
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a possibility of not suffering.

474
00:32:22,282 --> 00:32:22,922
[SPEAKER_01]: There is action.

475
00:32:23,022 --> 00:32:24,022
[SPEAKER_01]: There are results of action.

476
00:32:24,142 --> 00:32:27,263
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the extent of things that I cannot reasonably know.

477
00:32:27,883 --> 00:32:29,904
[SPEAKER_01]: I cannot reason next life or previous life.

478
00:32:31,004 --> 00:32:35,505
[SPEAKER_01]: But I can also not reason the belief of there is no next life and there is no previous life.

479
00:32:36,985 --> 00:32:43,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So you, like... Basically, the mundane right view is the transparency of reason.

480
00:32:44,574 --> 00:32:50,796
[SPEAKER_01]: When your reason is not used to, like, justify greed, aversion, delusion, but when your reason is self-honest.

481
00:32:52,096 --> 00:32:54,337
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what mundane right view is.

482
00:32:54,357 --> 00:32:58,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Honest, like, I know only to this extent, I don't know that extent.

483
00:32:59,358 --> 00:33:05,420
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean that I can now justifiably accept the view and belief for the extent that I don't know.

484
00:33:07,201 --> 00:33:10,802
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to recognize reasonably that I don't know this much.

485
00:33:12,027 --> 00:33:14,770
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you also start asking, well, is it important to know this much?

486
00:33:15,170 --> 00:33:16,191
[SPEAKER_01]: What is important to know?

487
00:33:18,754 --> 00:33:22,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the inevitable question would be, why do I want to know that?

488
00:33:23,258 --> 00:33:26,802
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unpleasant for me, the lack of proof, the lack of belief.

489
00:33:26,822 --> 00:33:28,663
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes me feel anxious, not knowing.

490
00:33:29,504 --> 00:33:29,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

491
00:33:30,365 --> 00:33:34,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is the factual reality that you can reasonably discern and accept.

492
00:33:35,095 --> 00:33:37,942
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent of not knowing certain things makes me feel anxious.

493
00:33:38,744 --> 00:33:39,686
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the next conclusion?

494
00:33:39,907 --> 00:33:41,009
[SPEAKER_01]: I am subject to anxiety.

495
00:33:41,491 --> 00:33:44,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I am truly my own, in control of myself,

496
00:33:45,479 --> 00:33:48,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing could cause anxiety in me, yet there are things that can cause anxiety in me.

497
00:33:49,263 --> 00:33:55,729
[SPEAKER_01]: So, will I use now my reason to cover up that anxiety and jump into a belief that there is no next life, we all die, we're just elements?

498
00:33:56,530 --> 00:34:04,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Or will I use my reason to basically negate the reason and just develop this view that everything will work itself out and everything will be fine and we are all heading to heaven?

499
00:34:05,679 --> 00:34:10,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, will I use the mundane right view, the right reason, and say I am subject to anxiety?

500
00:34:10,344 --> 00:34:12,046
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the hard truth.

501
00:34:12,567 --> 00:34:16,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Whichever way I take it, the first truth is I am subject to anxiety.

502
00:34:16,891 --> 00:34:18,433
[SPEAKER_01]: I am subject to suffering.

503
00:34:18,493 --> 00:34:19,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are not in my control.

504
00:34:20,717 --> 00:34:26,443
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you keep your reason on that transparent level, self-transparent level, you will look for the way out.

505
00:34:26,523 --> 00:34:27,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Existence is the problem.

506
00:34:27,625 --> 00:34:28,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is the problem.

507
00:34:28,946 --> 00:34:30,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is the problem.

508
00:34:30,328 --> 00:34:36,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said, like, suffering, becoming self-aware of suffering, rightly, can only result in two things.

509
00:34:37,335 --> 00:34:41,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Madness, losing your mind on account of anxiety, or noble search.

510
00:34:42,965 --> 00:34:46,610
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, okay, who can help me with this problem?

511
00:34:47,050 --> 00:34:50,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is a medicine that can basically cure me from this?

512
00:34:50,614 --> 00:34:53,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the necessary attitude for the right dhamma practice.

513
00:34:57,943 --> 00:34:59,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that anxiety is a...

514
00:35:00,346 --> 00:35:00,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a measure.

515
00:35:03,297 --> 00:35:12,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's why people jump into beliefs and faith and believe in next lives unreasonably and disbelieve in next lives unreasonably.

516
00:35:12,542 --> 00:35:14,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of the pain, the discomfort they're trying to get rid of.

517
00:35:15,003 --> 00:35:22,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, the reality is that you can, again, use your reason to, like, uncover is either I believe or I don't believe, I do it.

518
00:35:23,176 --> 00:35:24,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it displeases me.

519
00:35:24,337 --> 00:35:24,977
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

520
00:35:25,057 --> 00:35:25,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's why.

521
00:35:25,578 --> 00:35:26,058
[SPEAKER_01]: The basis.

522
00:35:26,498 --> 00:35:32,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So, rationally, you discern that you keep acting out of discomfort that you are subjected to.

523
00:35:33,142 --> 00:35:34,383
[SPEAKER_01]: You try to get rid of pain.

524
00:35:34,964 --> 00:35:36,164
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your ultimate value.

525
00:35:36,525 --> 00:35:37,245
[SPEAKER_01]: To not suffer.

526
00:35:37,585 --> 00:35:41,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you pursue the world or try to get rid of the world, you do it because you don't want to suffer.

527
00:35:42,648 --> 00:35:42,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

528
00:35:43,169 --> 00:35:50,714
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your recognition then stays on that level, then you actually are a step closer to understand the nature of suffering.

529
00:35:50,754 --> 00:35:52,015
[SPEAKER_01]: And, obviously, the escape from it.

530
00:35:53,243 --> 00:35:58,649
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, they will not require a belief, ultimately, and for its own sake.

531
00:35:59,210 --> 00:36:01,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Beliefs that sacrifice reason are wrong.

532
00:36:02,273 --> 00:36:08,780
[SPEAKER_01]: But beliefs that make you keep repeating that which you reason out rightly, that's a good belief.

533
00:36:08,800 --> 00:36:10,102
[SPEAKER_01]: That will actually make you do the work.

534
00:36:12,304 --> 00:36:13,766
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, faith is necessary.

535
00:36:13,786 --> 00:36:14,847
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the power of faith.

536
00:36:15,950 --> 00:36:21,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But faith is also dangerous, because if you haven't reasoned clearly enough, you can place it in something very wrong.

537
00:36:22,392 --> 00:36:24,332
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the peril of human situation.

538
00:36:24,392 --> 00:36:30,274
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot know you're right until you commit to that which seems right, and then keep applying it until you get the right results.

539
00:36:32,635 --> 00:36:34,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, which is...

540
00:36:36,317 --> 00:36:43,462
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, you can use any reason to recognize that, well, either way, you're subjected to suffering, subjected to anxiety, subjected to problem.

541
00:36:43,983 --> 00:36:46,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fundamentally, you have nothing to lose.

542
00:36:48,406 --> 00:36:55,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so you better apply something and make some effort to trial and error and hope you have enough time in this life to do the work.

